/-
# Groups generated by 3-transpositions are locally finite (Kourovka Notebook Problem 12.76)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 12.76 (A. I. Sozutov): is every group
generated by a set of 3-transpositions locally finite? A set of 3-transpositions is a normal set of
involutions whose pairwise products have order at most 3.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Lattice
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 12.76 (A. I.
Sozutov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700894 (`KOU-12.76`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A normal set is closed under conjugation by `G`; involutions are elements `d ≠ 1` with `d² = 1`;
order at most 3 is stated with explicit powers (`d e = 1`, `(d e)² = 1` or `(d e)³ = 1`), so that
elements of infinite order (`orderOf = 0`) are excluded. Locally finite means that every finitely
generated subgroup is finite.
-/

/-- `D` is a set of 3-transpositions: a normal (conjugation-invariant) set of involutions whose
pairwise products have order at most 3. -/
def IsThreeTranspositionSet {G : Type*} [Group G] (D : Set G) : Prop :=
  (∀ g : G, ∀ d ∈ D, g * d * g⁻¹ ∈ D) ∧ (∀ d ∈ D, d ≠ 1 ∧ d ^ 2 = 1) ∧
    ∀ d ∈ D, ∀ e ∈ D, d * e = 1 ∨ (d * e) ^ 2 = 1 ∨ (d * e) ^ 3 = 1

/-- `G` is locally finite: every finitely generated subgroup is finite. -/
def IsLocallyFinite (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∀ S : Set G, S.Finite → (Subgroup.closure S : Set G).Finite

/-- Kourovka 12.76 (A. I. Sozutov): every group generated by a set of 3-transpositions is locally
finite. -/
def threeTranspositionGroupLocallyFinite : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] (D : Set G), IsThreeTranspositionSet D → Subgroup.closure D = ⊤ →
    IsLocallyFinite G

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 12.76 "Yes, it is" (H. Cuypers and J. I. Hall, 1992).
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 12.76 (A. I.
Sozutov); resolution: H. Cuypers and J. I. Hall, in: Groups, combinatorics and geometry (Durham,
1990), London Math. Soc. Lecture Note Ser. 165, Cambridge Univ. Press, 1992, 121-138, Kourovka
Archive, Problem 12.76: Yes, it is.
-/
public theorem_wanted threeTranspositionGroupLocallyFinite_holds :
    threeTranspositionGroupLocallyFinite

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted
